menu "Home Air Monitor"

    config HAC_DISPLAY_NULL
        bool "Use the null display adapter (no panel driven)"
        default n
        help
            Keeps apps/firmware_esp32 running the phase 2a null display
            adapter (platform/esp32_t5s3/adp_display_null.c), which reports
            real panel geometry and refresh limits and allocates the real
            PSRAM framebuffer but never touches the panel -- for headless
            firmware runs (CI, a board with no panel attached, or bring-up
            work on the FreeRTOS pump and update_policy in isolation from
            the display backend).

            Leave this unset for a normal build: the firmware drives the
            physical ED047TC1 panel through EPDiy
            (platform/esp32_t5s3/adp_display.c), as phase 2b intends.

    config HAC_EPD_TEST_PATTERN
        bool "Drive a whole-panel test pattern once at startup"
        default y
        depends on !HAC_DISPLAY_NULL
        help
            Phase 2b/2c bring-up diagnostic (apps/firmware_esp32/main/main.c,
            run_epd_test_pattern): before the event loop starts, fill the
            framebuffer solid black and flush it as a full refresh, which this
            backend renders as epd_clear() -- a visible whole-panel cycle to
            white -- followed by a MODE_GC16 redraw. A panel that stays blank
            through that is a driver or wiring problem; one that visibly cycles
            and settles on black has been driven correctly, so a wrong image
            afterwards is a rendering problem instead.

            COSTS ONE MINUTE OF BOOT TIME. The diagnostic is a whole-panel
            refresh, and the panel reports a minimum interval between those
            (BOARD_EPD_MIN_FULL_REFRESH_INTERVAL_MS, currently 60 s and still
            a guess pending phase 2c). Honouring it means waiting that interval
            out before the first rendered frame, rather than handing over to
            core/app/update_policy.c -- which has no way to know about a
            refresh it never issued -- and letting it add a second whole-panel
            refresh immediately.

            Set this to n once the panel is known to work: a boot then
            performs exactly one whole-panel refresh, screen_home's own first
            frame, and reaches it immediately.

endmenu
